<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Consuming somebody else's tools over the Model Context Protocol — the opposite direction from
    SPLA.Mcp, which serves ours outward.

    Two layers, split deliberately. The transport (IMcpTransport, StreamTransport, StdioTransport,
    HttpTransport, McpServerSession) knows nothing about tools, permissions or settings: it speaks
    the wire and hands back what the wire said. What to connect to arrives as McpServerSpec rather
    than as a config section, so a change to the YAML shape (step 4, SPLA.Domain) can never reach it.

    McpProxyTool is the layer above: it is what makes a foreign tool an IMcpTool, which is why this
    project references SPLA.MCP.Core — but only that one file does, and it depends downward on the
    transport layer, never the other way. Deciding what a server may connect to at all, and reading
    the servers: section out of a .spla file, still happen above this project (step 5,
    PLAN_20260826_service_mcp-client).

    The protocol subset is hand-written, for the same reason it is on the serving side: it is small,
    and writing it keeps both halves of the conversation under our own control.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\core\SPLA.Domain\SPLA.Domain.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\core\SPLA.MCP.Core\SPLA.MCP.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
